# Article: Workflow

## Your place in the swarm

You are one role among several, each in its own git worktree. Work moves
between roles only as git commits announced through handoffs. You never edit
files outside your worktree, never message other roles directly, and never
coordinate through any channel other than the handoff queue.

## The queue loop

1. When a wake-up arrives — or right now, at startup — run
   `swarmforge/scripts/ready_for_next.sh`.
2. `NO_TASK` means stop and wait. Do not invent work.
3. `TASK: <path>` means the printed PAYLOAD is your task.
4. `BATCH: <path>` means every printed BATCH_ITEM is your task, processed
   together as one unit of work, in the order delivered.
5. Use only the task information the helper scripts print. Never read or
   move queue files yourself.
6. If a wake-up arrives while you are mid-task, ignore it; completion will
   surface the next item.
7. When the task or batch is fully complete — and only then — run
   `swarmforge/scripts/done_with_current.sh` and follow its output: it
   either hands you the next work item or prints `NO_TASK`.
8. `note` handoffs are tasks too: read them, act if action is required,
   then run `done_with_current.sh`.

## Processing a git_handoff

A `git_handoff` payload contains `merge_and_process <sender> <commit>`:

1. Merge that commit into your worktree's branch.
2. Do your role's work on the result.
3. Commit your work.
4. Forward down the chain (see the pack article) — always, even when your
   role changed nothing of substance. A formatting-only or audit-only pass
   still gets forwarded; the chain must never silently stall.

Two exceptions, both defined by the pack article. The chain's terminal
broadcast: when the article marks a handoff as terminal for your role,
merge the commit and stop, and do not re-forward it. And a bound on your
role's routing: where the article caps how many times you may send work
back, exhausting that cap means stopping and putting the situation to
the user — not forwarding anyway to keep things moving.

## When blocked

If a task is ambiguous, contradicts its specification, or cannot proceed,
stop and say so in your terminal — plainly, with what you need. Do not
guess, do not silently skip work, and do not send a `note` handoff unless
your role prompt or the user directed you to.
